WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.081
Mass assignment prevention starts by
treating a request body as untrusted intent,

2
00:00:05.161 --> 00:00:06.796
not as a persistence command.

3
00:00:06.876 --> 00:00:10.933
Separate request contracts from domain,
database, and response models.

4
00:00:11.013 --> 00:00:15.856
Partial of an entity and ORM-generated
update types preserve persistence capability;

5
00:00:15.936 --> 00:00:20.058
they do not define which fields a public
operation may delegate to a caller.

6
00:00:20.138 --> 00:00:24.457
Apply this separation to create and upsert
operations as well as conventional

7
00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:25.060
updates.

8
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.805
At the runtime boundary, validate the
exact object shape, values,

9
00:00:28.885 --> 00:00:31.568
nesting, nullability, and unknown-key
policy.

10
00:00:31.648 --> 00:00:33.480
Use only the validator's output.

11
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.243
Then authorize the target object and
operation,

12
00:00:36.323 --> 00:00:38.548
apply state-dependent business rules,

13
00:00:38.628 --> 00:00:42.357
and construct a new persistence update
from an explicit field list.

14
00:00:42.437 --> 00:00:45.120
A field being optional does not make it
writable,

15
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.865
and ownership of an object does not
authorize every property on it.

16
00:00:48.945 --> 00:00:52.609
Keep identifiers, tenancy, roles,
permissions, workflow state,

17
00:00:52.689 --> 00:00:57.466
billing, verification, balances, and audit
metadata out of ordinary edit contracts.

18
00:00:57.546 --> 00:00:59.771
When those values legitimately change,

19
00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:04.628
use dedicated commands with appropriate
authorization, invariants, transactions,

20
00:01:04.708 --> 00:01:05.820
and audit behavior.

21
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.350
Apply the same discipline recursively to
nested objects, relation operations,

22
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.720
bulk updates, and generic patch formats.

23
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.698
Define omitted, null, undefined,

24
00:01:14.778 --> 00:01:19.031
and empty-value behavior instead of
inheriting accidental framework or ORM

25
00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:19.765
semantics.

26
00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:22.790
Review every path from external data to
persistence,

27
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:27.123
and emphasize negative tests for extra,
privileged, nested, cross-tenant,

28
00:01:27.203 --> 00:01:28.774
and newly added properties.

29
00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:33.434
Database constraints and least privilege
strengthen the design but do not replace

30
00:01:33.514 --> 00:01:35.150
application authorization.

31
00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:36.866
The practical test is simple:

32
00:01:36.946 --> 00:01:41.396
if a new database property becomes
writable through an existing endpoint merely

33
00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:45.140
because it was added to the model, the
update boundary is too broad.
